WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.890
Corporate welfare.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.546
Billions to profitable corporations.

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:12.920
The federal government provides billions in subsidies, tax credits, loans, grants, and bailouts to Canadian corporations — many of which are profitable.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:18.647
Bombardier has received multiple government bailouts while paying executive bonuses and cutting jobs.

00:00:18.647 --> 00:00:24.238
SNC-Lavalin received government contracts after the PMO interfered in its prosecution.

00:00:24.238 --> 00:00:29.795
The auto sector has received billions in production subsidies including for EV battery plants.

00:00:29.795 --> 00:00:34.999
Oil and gas companies receive fossil fuel subsidies that Canada has pledged to eliminate.

00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:46.818
Each subsidy is a policy choice: public money directed to private corporations rather than to the public services documented as failing across this site — healthcare, housing, Indigenous communities, military families.

00:00:46.818 --> 00:00:52.522
The PBO and AG have both examined corporate subsidy programs and questioned their effectiveness.

00:00:52.522 --> 00:00:58.863
The lobbying infrastructure documented on this site ensures that subsidies continue flowing regardless of outcomes.

00:00:58.863 --> 00:01:02.272
Documented cases of corporate welfare in Canada.

00:01:02.272 --> 00:01:04.329
The subsidy pipeline.

00:01:04.329 --> 00:01:09.193
Corporate welfare is the fiscal mechanism that connects lobbying to policy outcomes.

00:01:09.193 --> 00:01:11.784
Corporations invest in lobbying.

00:01:11.784 --> 00:01:16.113
Lobbying secures subsidies, tax credits, and preferential treatment.

00:01:16.113 --> 00:01:24.159
The subsidies are funded by taxpayer revenue — which as documented in the tax policy analysis is disproportionately collected from wage earners.

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:26.749
The corporations report profits.

00:01:26.749 --> 00:01:29.318
Executives receive bonuses.

00:01:29.318 --> 00:01:31.397
And the cycle continues.

00:01:31.397 --> 00:01:44.522
Meanwhile, the public services documented as failing across this site — healthcare with six point five million without a doctor, military with sixteen thousand personnel shortfall, Indigenous communities with boil water advisories — remain underfunded.

00:01:44.522 --> 00:01:49.136
Every dollar of corporate welfare is a dollar not spent on public services.

00:01:49.136 --> 00:01:54.738
The choice to subsidise profitable corporations while public services fail is not accidental.

00:01:54.738 --> 00:02:00.136
It is the documented output of the lobbying infrastructure that maintains institutional capture.
